{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Who is like You among the mighty ones, YHWH.</b> In tractate Gittin (56b) they expounded 'Who is like You among the silent ones (בָּאִלְּמִים),' and this derash seems far-fetched to read 'among the mighty ones (בָּאֵלִים)' as 'among the silent ones (בָּאִלְּמִים)' by adding a mem. However, it appears that the Sages, of blessed memory, did not intend to add a mem, but rather 'among the mighty ones (בָּאֵלִים)' means among the strong, as Rashi explained. And this is its meaning: for anyone who hears his shame and restrains himself to be as one who is silent and suppresses his anger — behold, he is strong and mighty, for 'Who is a hero? One who conquers his inclination.' So too, when the Holy One, blessed be He, hears and is silent and makes Himself as though mute, as it were, this is from the side of His strong hand, blessed be He, that He suppresses His anger and is silent toward those who provoke Him — as He was silent toward Pharaoh when he said 'מִי ה׳' (Exodus 5:2), and likewise He was silent toward Titus for his blasphemy and mockery. Therefore they expounded 'מִי כָמוֹךָ בָּאֵלִים' — 'among the silent ones (בָּאִלְּמִים).' And Moses said this concerning the blasphemy of Pharaoh, and from it they brought this verse as proof concerning Titus. And in any case the meaning of 'among the mighty ones (בָּאֵלִים)' is literally among the strong, as stated." "And if you wish to say it means literally 'among the silent ones (בָּאִלְּמִים),' we may explain that Moses said that His praise, blessed be He, is so very great that even the mute, who have no mouth to speak — nevertheless His praise, blessed be He, ascends from them as though they said with a full mouth 'Who is like You, YHWH,' in the manner that it says (Psalms 19:2) 'הַשָּׁמַיִם מְסַפְּרִים כְּבוֹד אֵל' etc., 'אֵין אֹמֶר וְאֵין דְּבָרִים בְּלִי נִשְׁמָע קוֹלָם' etc. And if so, how do they recount the glory of El? Rather, surely it is through the fact that creatures observe the movements of the heavens and all their host, that they recount the glory of El. And through the fact that creatures recount the glory of El by means of the movements of the heavens, it is as though the heavens themselves are recounting — even though in truth they are mute and there is in them no utterance or words. Therefore it says here 'מִי כָמֹכָה בָּאֵלִים' — among the mute: even from the mouth of the mute Your praise is great, as though they said 'Who is like You, YHWH,' and all the more so from the mouth of those who speak." "And he said this also concerning the waters, in the manner of what is brought in Bereshit Rabbah (5:1) on 'יִקָּווּ הַמַּיִם' — a parable to a king who built a palace and settled in it mute tenants, and they would rise early and greet the king. Afterward he settled in it intelligent tenants, and they rose and rebelled against the king, etc. The king said: Let the palace return to its former state. So too, from the beginning of the world's creation, the praise of the Holy One, blessed be He, came from the waters, as it says (Psalms 93:4) 'מִקֹּלוֹת מַיִם רַבִּים אַדִּירִים מִשְׁבְּרֵי יָם, אַדִּיר בַּמָּרוֹם ה׳' etc. And according to this midrash the waters are called mute, and His praise, blessed be He, ascends from them — whether through the fact that they do the will of their Creator contrary to their nature, or through the fact that creatures recount the glory of El when they observe the deeds of the waters that they performed in the generation of the Flood and in the splitting of the Sea of Reeds — and it is as though the waters were recounting the glory of El even though they are mute. Therefore Moses said, 'מִי כָמֹכָה בָּאֵלִם ה׳' — even the mute say 'Who is like You.' And he said this concerning what the mute waters did at the splitting of the Sea of Reeds. And what is written 'בָּאֵלִם' lacking a mem is in order to expound 'בָּאֵלִם' as well in the sense of 'the strong,' an allusion to mighty waters, for 'אַדִּירִים' is an expression of strength, like 'בָּאֵלִם' which is an expression of strength, as it is written (Exodus 15:10) 'צָלֲלוּ כַּעוֹפֶרֶת בְּמַיִם אַדִּירִים' — and this is a precious interpretation. And what is expounded concerning Titus, that this was applied to him — is because a wave of the sea rose up to drown him, as is concluded there in the Gemara; and with this understanding, the meaning of all the derashot is one and the same: to expound 'mighty ones (אֵלִים)' as 'the strong' and 'mute ones (אִלְּמִים)' together." "Another matter: since anyone who multiplies in recounting His praise, blessed be He, is called one who diminishes — as Rashi explained on the verse 'נוֹרָא תְהִלֹּת' (Exodus 15:11) — therefore it says 'מִי כָמֹכָה בָּאֵלִם,' that is, among the strong who suppress themselves from recounting Your praise out of fear lest they diminish Your praise — therefore they make themselves as though mute. And among these classes, 'Who is like You, YHWH,' for 'לְךָ דֻמִיָּה תְהִלָּה' (Psalms 65:2) — silence itself is Your praise. And according to this, 'בָּאֵלִם' is expounded as well: among the strong and among the mute, for by making themselves as mute, it is likened through this silence as though they say 'Who is like You, YHWH.' And the reason they make themselves mute is because You are 'נוֹרָא תְהִלֹּת' — fearful in praises; they are afraid to declare Your praise lest they diminish it, therefore they make themselves as mute, and with the strength of the hand they suppress their mouth, placing a hand over the mouth. And this too is a precious interpretation."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:20:42.392258629Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}